  "content": {
    "id": "p140-group17-20-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "Find the range of the following.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p140-group17-20-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt{x^2+4}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$[2,\\infty)$",
          "worked": "As $x$ varies over $\\mathbb R$, squaring gives every non-negative value.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2 &\\in [0,\\infty)\\\\x^2+4 &\\in [4,\\infty)\\\\\\sqrt{x^2+4} &\\in [2,\\infty).\\end{align*}$$\nThe minimum $2$ occurs at $x=0$, and every larger output is attained. The range is $[2,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p140-group17-20-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt{x^2-4}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$[0,\\infty)$",
          "worked": "The square root requires a non-negative radicand.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-4 &\\ge 0\\\\x &\\in (-\\infty,-2]\\cup[2,\\infty).\\end{align*}$$\nOn this domain, the radicand takes every non-negative value.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-4 &\\in [0,\\infty)\\\\\\sqrt{x^2-4} &\\in [0,\\infty).\\end{align*}$$\nThe output is $0$ at $x=\\pm2$, and every positive output is attained. The range is $[0,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p140-group17-20-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(0,\\infty)$",
          "worked": "The square root must exist and the denominator must be non-zero, so $x>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x &\\in (0,\\infty)\\\\\\sqrt{x} &\\in (0,\\infty)\\\\\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} &\\in (0,\\infty).\\end{align*}$$\nThe square-root outputs cover all positive values. Reciprocating them gives every positive value and never zero. The range is $(0,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p140-group17-20-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\frac{1}{1+x^2}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(0,1]$",
          "worked": "As $x$ varies over $\\mathbb R$, squaring gives every non-negative value.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2 &\\in [0,\\infty)\\\\1+x^2 &\\in [1,\\infty)\\\\\\frac{1}{1+x^2} &\\in (0,1].\\end{align*}$$\nThe maximum is $f(0)=1$. The denominator can grow without bound, so the output approaches $0$ but never equals it. Every value between $0$ and $1$ is attained. The range is $(0,1]$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      }
    ]
  },
